---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Account kinds and addresses">
  <h1>Account kinds and addresses</h1>
  <p>
    A project can issue every account an <strong>account address</strong> on a
    domain the project owns. People keep signing up with whatever they have, an
    email at any provider or a username, and each account also gets an address
    on your domain: a project-unique handle for every account. Identity issues
    and stores the address; it does not deliver mail. A project that runs mail
    for the domain routes it by reading <code>account_address</code> from its
    users. A released address can be issued again straight away, so a mail
    system should key mailboxes on the user id.
  </p>

  <h2>Which accounts a project has, and who creates them</h2>
  <p>
    A project has two kinds of account, and chooses for each who creates it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Email accounts</strong>, identified by an email at any provider:
      <code>accounts.email_signup</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Username accounts</strong>, identified by a username and a
      password, with no email: <code>accounts.username_signup</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each setting takes one of three values:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>self</code>: people create their own. This is the default for email
      accounts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>admin</code>: only an admin creates them, through
      <code>CreateUser</code> (and <code>InviteUser</code> for email accounts).
      People sign in to the account an admin made, and self-signup is refused
      as invitation-only. Sign-in codes go only to addresses that already have
      an account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>off</code>: the project has no accounts of this kind to create, and
      both self-signup and admin creation are refused. This is the default for
      username accounts. Accounts of the kind that already exist keep signing
      in.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code lang="json" code={`{
  "access": { "mode": "open" },
  "accounts": {
    "domain": "accounts.example.com",
    "email_signup": "self",
    "username_signup": "self"
  }
}`} />
  <p>
    A consumer product keeps email self-signup and, if it wants handles, turns
    on username self-signup. A workplace sets <code>email_signup</code> to
    <code>off</code> and <code>username_signup</code> to <code>admin</code>, so
    every account is one its admins made, with an address on its domain. The
    default project takes these from
    <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_EMAIL_SIGNUP</code> and
    <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_USERNAME_SIGNUP</code>. Managed child accounts
    are a guardian's to create and are not governed by these settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    The access mode still applies. A username has no email for an allowlist or
    a deny-list to match, so username self-signup works only in
    <code>open</code> mode: <code>invite</code> refuses it as invitation-only,
    and <code>allowlist</code> and <code>closed</code> refuse it.
  </p>

  <h3>Username accounts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      People sign up with <code>UsernameSignup</code> (username, password, and
      optionally a name, date of birth and market) and sign in with
      <code>PasswordLogin</code>, using the username as the identifier.
    </li>
    <li>
      An admin creates one with <code>CreateUser</code>, setting
      <code>username</code> instead of <code>email</code>. The response carries
      a temporary password to hand to the person.
    </li>
    <li>
      A username is 3–32 lowercase letters, digits, <code>_</code>,
      <code>-</code> and <code>.</code>. It may not contain <code>-at-</code>,
      start or end with <code>.</code>, or contain <code>..</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A username is a public handle, so a taken one is reported as taken.
    </li>
    <li>
      A date of birth in the child band is refused. A child's account is made by
      their guardian, with consent, as a managed child account.
    </li>
    <li>
      A username account has no email, so it has no email-based password reset.
      An admin resets its password.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Turning on addresses</h2>
  <p>
    Set <code>accounts.domain</code> in the project's config. The default
    project takes it from <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_EMAIL_DOMAIN</code>. Leaving it
    empty, the default, issues no addresses.
  </p>
  <Code lang="json" code={`{
  "accounts": {
    "domain": "accounts.example.com"
  }
}`} />
  <p>
    The domain is stored lower-cased, without a trailing dot and IDN-punycoded,
    and must be a fully qualified name. A domain that fails these checks fails
    the config write. Mail reaches the addresses only if the domain's MX records
    point at a mail system that delivers them. Issuing an address does not set
    up mail.
  </p>

  <h2>How an address is formed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Username accounts</strong> get <code>&lt;username&gt;@&lt;domain&gt;</code>:
      <code>bob</code> becomes <code>bob@accounts.example.com</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Email accounts</strong> keep their whole email, with the
      <code>@</code> written as <code>-at-</code>:
      <code>bob@mail.example</code> becomes
      <code>bob-at-mail.example@accounts.example.com</code>. Two people named Bob
      at different providers get different addresses, and the plain
      <code>bob@accounts.example.com</code> stays free for a username account.
      An email already on the account domain takes the same form: the plain
      namespace belongs to usernames, so a username and an email never claim
      the same address.
    </li>
    <li>
      Letters are lower-cased. Every character other than <code>a–z</code>,
      <code>0–9</code>, <code>.</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>
      becomes <code>-</code>, and runs of dots collapse, so the address is
      always valid. That includes <code>+</code>: mail systems that use
      subaddressing would deliver <code>bob+news-at-…</code> to
      <code>bob</code>. Different identifiers can therefore map to the same
      address; the later account takes a suffix.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the part before the <code>@</code> is longer than 64 characters (the
      limit for an email address), it keeps its first characters and ends in
      eight hex digits of a hash of the whole value. The same account always
      gets the same address.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the address is already taken, the account gets the next free suffix:
      <code>-2</code>, <code>-3</code> and so on. After twenty, a suffix
      derived from the account's id ends the search.
    </li>
    <li>
      New usernames may not contain <code>-at-</code>, so no new username can
      take the address another person's email derives to. Usernames created
      before this rule keep working as they are.
    </li>
    <li>Anonymous accounts get no address until they are upgraded.</li>
    <li>
      Role names that speak for a domain, such as <code>admin</code>,
      <code>postmaster</code>, <code>hostmaster</code>, <code>abuse</code> and
      <code>webmaster</code>, are never issued. New usernames may not be one,
      and a username stored earlier that is one gets an address with a suffix
      derived from the account's id instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      An email-derived address contains the person's email. Treat it with the
      same visibility rules as the email, and never as proof of it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When it is issued</h2>
  <p>
    A username (managed child) account gets its address when its guardian
    creates it. An email account gets its address once its email is verified:
    at sign-up when the sign-up proves the address (OAuth, passkey, an emailed
    code), otherwise at the first sign-in or session refresh after
    verification. That includes accounts an admin invites and accounts SCIM
    provisions. An address spells the email, so it is never issued for an
    email nobody has proven they own. An account that existed before the
    project set a domain gets its address at its next sign-in or session
    refresh, once its email is verified.
  </p>
  <p>
    An address is written only to an account that has none, so it is never
    replaced behind the account's back, and changing the project's domain
    leaves issued addresses as they were. An address does follow a change of
    the identifier it came from, so it never goes on spelling out a name or
    mailbox the person gave up: an email account's follows a change of email,
    confirmed by the person or written by SCIM, and a managed child's follows
    its guardian's rename. A project
    that stops issuing addresses keeps the ones it issued. An address released
    by an email change can later be issued to another account, so treat the
    address as a handle and key on the user id. Failing to
    store an address never blocks a sign-in: the server logs it and tries again
    at the next sign-in.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it appears</h2>
  <p>
    <code>User.account_address</code> carries it on every <code>User</code> the
    API returns: sign-up, sign-in and refresh, <code>GetCurrentUser</code>, the
    admin user reads, a guardian's view of a managed child, and the data
    export.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
